{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Santa Fe's Houston-to-transcontinental passenger train, eventually named The California Special, which connected Houston (and, via a through sleeper, New Orleans) to the Chicago-Los Angeles transcontinental trains at Clovis, NM, over the Gulf, Colorado and Santa Fe / Panhandle and Santa Fe trackage drawn in the companion file data/gulf_colorado_santa_fe_railway.geojson. Modeled after the structure of the existing data/santa_fe_chief.geojson (The Chief / Super Chief / El Capitan as their own named-train features layered on top of general Santa Fe trackage). This train predates Amtrak (May 1, 1971) throughout its documented life and was discontinued in July 1968, so no Amtrak-era segment is drawn.", "sources": ["https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track9/califspecial195407.html", "https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track9/califspecial194408.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Texas_Express", "https://browncountyhistory.org/passenger_trains.html", "data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson (existing layer, same P&SF company/color)"], "geometry_sources": ["Geometry traced from OpenStreetMap via Overpass (overpass-api.de, curl POST), railway=rail/abandoned/disused/razed ways in regional bounding boxes covering each named leg. Ways were assembled into a real-topology graph keyed by OSM node id (nodes within ~11 m / 4 decimal places merged), edges weighted by haversine length times a corridor-distance penalty (quadratic beyond 1.5 km from a polyline through the documented station towns) times an operator weight favoring BNSF/Santa Fe/Panhandle-tagged trackage over crossing lines (Union Pacific, CPKC, Texas & Pacific, Missouri Pacific, Southern Pacific), then routed with Dijkstra between the geocoded (Nominatim, structured city/state/country query) endpoint towns, restricted to the largest connected component. Raw paths were checked for self-intersection/backtracking using cumulative along-path distance (point pairs >=600 m apart along the path whose direct distance is <250 m are flagged); flagged out-and-back yard/dead-end spikes were spliced out, then the result was Douglas-Peucker simplified at 15 m. Final backtrack check: zero flags on every feature below. This is today's BNSF alignment (the corridor is understood to be largely unrelocated since original construction, but no period valuation map was consulted)."], "not_yet_sourced": "The exact branding of this service between its 1915 start and the well-documented 1954 'California Special' name is not established -- see the first segment's note for what was and was not found (a documented 'The Texan' name from Feb 1915, and an unbranded/numbered August 1944 timetable). The exact July 1968 discontinuance date has a several-day source conflict (July 18 vs July 21), recorded rather than resolved. avg_speed_kmh for the 1915-1954 segment is not computed (no full schedule sourced for that era) and the file's top-level avg_speed_kmh field is a blend of a real sourced figure (1954-1968) and an absent one (1915-1954) -- read the per-segment speed notes, not the feature-level figure, for what is actually sourced.", "pulled": "20260826"}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railway", "line": "Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe: California Special", "id": "atsf-california-special", "color": "#003DA5", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 55, "speed_source": "Blended across both segments below; see each segment's own speed note. The 1954-1968 segment's figure is a real sourced computation from the July 1954 Official Guide; the 1915-1954 segment's figure is an unsourced estimate.", "segments": [{"start": 1915, "end": 1954, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Through Houston-Clovis passenger service began once both the Coleman Cutoff (Brownwood-Sweetwater, opened March 1914) and the Lubbock-Texico Cut Off (opened March 1, 1914) were in place, connecting at Clovis with the transcontinental Chicago-Los Angeles trains. A specific named service, 'The Texan' (applied to the eastbound Clovis-to-Temple run), is documented from February 7, 1915 -- used here as the start date for this segment, matching the same February 7, 1915 date already cited in data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson for 'the California Special' on this corridor. Whether the whole route carried a single consistent name between 1915 and the 1950s is NOT well established: an August 1944 Official Guide page for this run (streamlinerschedules.com) is headed 'Texas-California Passenger Service' and lists the equipment by train number (6-75-92-97-23 westbound / 24-98-91-76-5 eastbound) rather than by a single train name, with a 'Grand Canyon Limited' mention appearing only in an equipment/car-routing note, not as this train's own name. The 'California Special' name is only clearly, consistently documented from the July 1954 timetable onward (see the next segment). This segment is therefore drawn under the 'California Special' label for continuity with the rest of this feature and with data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson's usage, but the exact branding through 1915-1954 is honestly uncertain -- treat the name as a convenience label for the service, not a fully sourced historical train name for this whole span. New Orleans-Houston through cars (via Missouri Pacific per the calling session's prior research; the 1944 timetable itself shows Santa Fe's own New Orleans-Houston coach and dining car rather than a documented Missouri Pacific interchange) ran alongside the Houston-Clovis segment for at least part of this era.", "sources": ["Segment-level: the February 7, 1915 date is carried over from data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson's own metadata (itself citing Wikipedia's West Texas Express article and tshaonline.org handbook entries); the 1944 timetable heading/train-numbering and the absence of a clear single train name that year are from a direct fetch of streamlinerschedules.com's August 1944 California Special page. headway_min (1440, once daily) is an estimate; avg_speed_kmh is not computed for this segment (no full-schedule source consulted for the pre-1954 era).", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Texas_Express", "data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson (existing layer, same P&SF company/color)", "https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track9/califspecial194408.html"]}, {"start": 1954, "end": 1968, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "By July 1954 the Official Guide clearly and consistently names this service 'The California Special' (trains 65/66/75/76), running Houston-Clovis daily, 658.1 miles, connecting at Clovis with through cars exchanged with the Grand Canyon and San Francisco Chief onward to Los Angeles/San Francisco, with a through New Orleans-Houston sleeping car and coach. 1954 westbound (train 66-75): Houston depart 6:45 PM, Clovis arrive 10:30 AM next day, 15h45m, 41.8 mph average. Eastbound (train 76-65): 15h50m, 41.6 mph average -- these two figures average to 67.1 km/h, used as this segment's avg_speed_kmh, a real computed figure, not a guess. Discontinued July 1968: one source (browncountyhistory.org, echoing the date already used in data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson) gives July 18, 1968; another (a web search summarizing additional Brown County sourcing) gives 'the last passenger train through Brownwood left the station July 21, 1968' -- both dates are recorded here rather than silently picking one, per CLAUDE.md Rule 3. This was before Amtrak (May 1, 1971), so no Amtrak-era segment is drawn for this train.", "sources": ["Segment-level: all mileposts, times and connection details are read directly from the July 1954 Official Guide page at streamlinerschedules.com (fetched for this reply). The July 1968 discontinuance is from browncountyhistory.org (July 18) and a second web-search-summarized source (July 21); both are recorded as a source conflict rather than silently picking one. avg_speed_kmh (67 km/h) is computed directly from the sourced 658.1-mile, 15h45m/15h50m timings -- a real sourced figure, not an estimate. headway_min (1440, once daily) matches the single daily train pair in the timetable.", "https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track9/califspecial195407.html", "https://browncountyhistory.org/passenger_trains.html", "data/santa_fe_amarillo_lubbock.geojson (existing layer, same P&SF company/color)"]}], "geometry_source": "Geometry traced from OpenStreetMap via Overpass (overpass-api.de, curl POST), railway=rail/abandoned/disused/razed ways in regional bounding boxes covering each named leg. Ways were assembled into a real-topology graph keyed by OSM node id (nodes within ~11 m / 4 decimal places merged), edges weighted by haversine length times a corridor-distance penalty (quadratic beyond 1.5 km from a polyline through the documented station towns) times an operator weight favoring BNSF/Santa Fe/Panhandle-tagged trackage over crossing lines (Union Pacific, CPKC, Texas & Pacific, Missouri Pacific, Southern Pacific), then routed with Dijkstra between the geocoded (Nominatim, structured city/state/country query) endpoint towns, restricted to the largest connected component. Raw paths were checked for self-intersection/backtracking using cumulative along-path distance (point pairs >=600 m apart along the path whose direct distance is <250 m are flagged); flagged out-and-back yard/dead-end spikes were spliced out, then the result was Douglas-Peucker simplified at 15 m. Final backtrack check: zero flags on every feature below. This is today's BNSF alignment (the corridor is understood to be largely unrelocated since original construction, but no period valuation map was consulted).", "geometry_quality": "Reuses the same real-traced OpenStreetMap geometry as the track-ownership features in data/gulf_colorado_santa_fe_railway.geojson (concatenation of the Houston-Temple, Temple-Brownwood, Coleman Cutoff, Sweetwater-Lubbock and Lubbock-Clovis traces, re-checked for backtracking after concatenation and cleaned of two small yard-junction spikes at Sweetwater and Lubbock). Traced length 650.9 mi against the July 1954 Official Guide's own documented Houston-Clovis distance of 658.1 mi -- a 1.1% undershoot, an excellent match given this is a ~650-mile corridor assembled from five independently-routed legs. See data/gulf_colorado_santa_fe_railway.geojson's per-leg geometry_quality notes for full per-town distance figures on each constituent leg. Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 4 ruled segments totalling 46 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed).", "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-101.6251, 33.43443], "to": [-101.77192, 33.5174], "chord_mi": 10.37, "traced_mi": 10.23, "snap_m": [1, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["BNSF Slaton Subdivision (10.23 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-102.1215, 33.77578], "to": [-102.29737, 33.90436], "chord_mi": 13.64, "traced_mi": 13.45, "snap_m": [0, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["BNSF Slaton Subdivision (13.45 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-100.01809, 32.4078], "to": [-100.19042, 32.46308], "chord_mi": 10.84, "traced_mi": 10.76, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["BNSF Lampasas Subdivision (10.76 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-96.12953, 29.68671], "to": [-96.16999, 29.83936], "chord_mi": 10.81, "traced_mi": 10.84, "snap_m": [0, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["BNSF Galveston Subdivision (10.84 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-95.36599, 29.76722], [-95.37173, 29.76874], [-95.39844, 29.7